1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,720
Maps can contain a lot of information,

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:09,880
so it’s important to guide readers’ attention
to the most important elements. In this video,

3
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:15,560
I’m going to explain how to do that by
establishing what’s called visual hierarchy.

4
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:21,000
The basic idea here is very simple: how much
attention readers give to different elements

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,440
on a map depends on the visual appearance
of that element and the ones surrounding it.

6
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:32,920
As an example, I’ll use this map of
personal robberies in San Francisco.

7
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:38,320
On this basic version of the map there are
two layers: one showing hotspots of robbery,

8
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:44,280
and a base map that helps readers
understand where those hotspots are.

9
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:50,080
On any map the most important layer is the
one showing the data we are interested in.

10
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:56,720
Because that layer is the most important, it
should also be what’s most visually prominent.

11
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:02,560
In this case, I’ve achieved that by using a strong
colour for the robbery layer while choosing a base

12
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:08,800
map that uses shades of grey. Since brighter,
more saturated colours – or colours that have

13
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:14,200
a greater contrast from the background – are
more visually prominent than shades of grey,

14
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:19,360
this helps the robbery hotspots
stand out from the background map.

15
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:25,360
Colour is just one way that we can influence
the visual prominence of elements on our map.

16
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:31,280
We can see this if we add some supporting
elements to this map, such as a title and

17
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:36,480
the names of local police districts. Now there
are several different types of text on the map,

18
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:43,307
it will be useful to help our readers understand
which is most important. You can see here that I

19
00:01:43,307 --> 00:01:49,120
have used text size to make the map title more
prominent than the text on the map itself,

20
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:52,240
which is in-turn more prominent
than the copyright statements at

21
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:57,200
the bottom. I’ve also made some of
the text a lighter shade of grey,

22
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:03,800
which makes it less visually prominent by reducing
the contrast between the text and the background.

23
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:08,360
Another way to increase or decrease the
visual prominence of a map element is to

24
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:13,760
use isolation. Elements that are isolated
from their surroundings, such as text that

25
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:19,480
is surrounded by a box of a contrasting colour,
or points that are surrounded by a thick line,

26
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:25,600
are more visually prominent than the same
element without that isolation. Similarly,

27
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,840
if an item is semi-transparent, that will
tend to reduce its visual prominence.

28
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:34,960
It can be useful to combine
attributes such as size,

29
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:41,200
colour, isolation and transparency to create
multiple levels of visual prominence on a map,

30
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:45,920
helping to guide readers to the
content we think is most important.

31
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:51,200
Whenever we are making decisions about how
to represent different features on a map,

32
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:57,680
it’s really important to make sure that the data
layer or layers is the most visually prominent,

33
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:03,360
since that is the layer readers need to focus
on most to make decisions. All the supporting

34
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:08,920
elements on a map – administrative boundaries,
base maps, labels, copyright information and so

35
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:15,440
on – should be less visually prominent than
the data layer to avoid distracting from it.

36
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:17,280
So in summary,

37
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:23,000
Creating a visual hierarchy helps guide
readers through the maps that we make

38
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,240
We can establish visual hierarchy
using combinations of size,

39
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:29,760
colour, is isolation and transparency

40
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:40,600
We should always make sure that the data layer
is the most visually prominent part of our maps